# Computational Biology Diaries — Episode 1

## **I Thought I Was Opening an Image. Turns Out I Opened a Living Embryo.**

There are days in life when you accidentally click on something and it changes how you think forever.

Today was one of those days.

A few weeks ago, if someone had shown me this:

```text
6bba_cf35214c.zarr
```

I would've confidently said,

> "That's... definitely a zip file."

I would've been wrong.

Very wrong.

Apparently, biology likes making software engineers question their entire existence.

---

When I started exploring developmental biology datasets, I had a beautifully simple mental model.

Microscope → Image → AI → Problem solved.

That's how computer vision works, right?

Right?

...

Not even close.

---

My first challenge wasn't AI.

It wasn't biology.

It wasn't even Python.

It was trying to answer one embarrassingly simple question.

> **"Where is the image?"**

Every normal dataset in the world behaves itself.

You double-click it.

An image opens.

Everyone is happy.

This dataset looked at that convention and politely said,

> "We don't do that here."

Instead, I found myself staring at something called a **Zarr** store.

Inside it...

A file called `zarr.json`.

And another file named...

`0`

Just...

`0`.

No extension.

No explanation.

No emotional support.

For a solid five minutes I genuinely wondered whether some scientist had accidentally leaned on their keyboard while naming files.

---

The funny thing is...

The more confused I became, the more curious I got.

Why would researchers invent an entirely different way of storing images?

The answer was surprisingly beautiful.

Because these aren't really images.

They're experiments.

---

That sentence deserves repeating.

They're not photographs.

They're experiments frozen in time.

Actually...

Not even frozen.

They're alive.

---

Eventually I managed to open one dataset.

Instead of seeing an image...

Python showed me this.

```python
(100, 64, 256, 256)
```

Four numbers.

That's it.

No embryo.

No cells.

No microscope.

Just four numbers staring back at me like they knew something I didn't.

Which, to be fair...

They did.

---

After a bit of digging, those numbers suddenly stopped looking random.

They became a story.

```text
100 time points

↓

64 optical slices

↓

256 × 256 pixels
```

And then it hit me.

I wasn't looking at one image.

I was looking at **6,400 images** hiding inside a single embryo.

No wonder the file refused to behave like a JPEG.

---

At this point I thought,

"Okay... surely now I'll finally see the embryo."

Python had other ideas.

Instead it printed something like this.

```python
[[18, 14, 18, ...],
 [26, 23, 26, ...],
 ...
]
```

Thousands of numbers.

Again.

Apparently biology's favorite hobby is reminding us that computers don't actually see pictures.

They see mathematics.

Humans see glowing cells.

Computers see matrices.

Somehow that tiny realization completely changed how I think about computer vision.

---

Then came *the moment.*

The one that made the last several days worth it.

One tiny command.

```python
plt.imshow(...)
```

And suddenly...

There it was.

Not a textbook illustration.

Not an animation.

A real fluorescence microscopy image from a developing zebrafish embryo.

I actually sat there staring at it longer than I'd like to admit.

Not because it was visually spectacular.

But because I finally understood what I was looking at.

Those bright blurry blobs weren't random white dots.

They were nuclei.

Real nuclei.

Inside a living embryo.

For the first time, all those conversations about developmental biology, microscopy, fluorescence, stem cells and cell lineage stopped being abstract ideas.

They became pixels.

---

Ironically...

The biggest lesson from today wasn't biological.

It was philosophical.

I started this journey thinking AI begins with models.

I'm beginning to suspect AI actually begins with understanding your data so well that you stop seeing files...

...and start seeing biology.

---

### What's Next?

Now that I've managed to convince one embryo to reveal itself, I'm not stopping there.

Next, I want to build my own interactive embryo explorer.

Not because it looks cool (although it definitely will).

Because I want to answer questions like:

* What happens if I scroll through all 64 depth slices?
* Can I literally watch the embryo developing over time?
* Why do some nuclei suddenly disappear?
* Did they move... or did I just change the focal plane?

And eventually...

Can I teach a computer to answer those questions better than I can?

I have absolutely no idea where this journey is going.

But one thing is certain.

If the first lesson taught me anything...

It's never trust a file named `0`.

